      "text": "State of Autonomous AI Agents (2026)\n1. Executive Overview\nAutonomous AI agents are software systems powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) that can reason,\nbreak down complex goals into sub-tasks, execute tools sequentially or in parallel, and observe execution\noutcomes. In 2026, enterprise adoption of agentic workflows surged by 142% compared to previous years.\nThe predominant architectures have shifted from rigid DAGs to dynamic tool-calling loops with strict\nschema-enforced structured outputs.\n2. Key Architectural Patterns\nModern agent design emphasizes typed dependency injection, deterministic schema validation using\nPydantic, and hybrid retrieval. Rather than relying solely on web search or static RAG, dual-engine agents\ncombine local domain knowledge bases (via PDF/vector stores) with live web grounding. Performance\nevaluations demonstrate that grounding reduces hallucination rates from 18.4% to under 2.1% in technical\ndomains.",
      "page": 1,
      "source": "sample_research.pdf",
      "chunk_id": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "3. Benchmark Numbers & Metrics\nOn the AgentBench-2026 benchmark, tool-use accuracy reached 94.6% when models were supplied with\nconcise, pre-sanitized tool outputs. Latency optimization via specialized inference hardware (such as Groq\nLPUs) achieved median response times under 320ms per token generation. Total multi-turn research\nworkflows averaged 4.2 tool invocations per user inquiry.\n4. Open Challenges\nKey unsolved challenges remain around long-context drift, cross-tool authentication security, and graceful\ndegradation during external API rate-limiting events. Future work focuses on asynchronous multi-agent\ncoordination protocols and Model Context Protocol (MCP) integrations.",
      "page": 2,
      "source": "sample_research.pdf",
      "chunk_id": 1
    }
  ],
